{
	title: 'System clipboard: copy, cut and paste',
	spec: `In the terminal, Cmd-C copies and Cmd-X cuts the prompt selection to the system clipboard, and Ctrl-V (and Cmd-V where the terminal passes it on) pastes from it; most terminals paste Cmd-V themselves as a bracketed paste, which already arrives as a paste key. The client writes with pbcopy on macOS and wl-copy or xclip on Linux, and with OSC 52 when it runs over SSH (SSH_TTY set), where the useful clipboard is the user's own machine; it reads with pbpaste, wl-paste or xclip. Clipboard I/O sits behind one client object that tests replace; a missing tool shows a notice and never throws or blocks the keys.
Pasted text, from either source, is cleaned: CRLF and CR become LF, control characters other than tab and newline are dropped. A paste is one undo step and replaces the selection.
Web: the browser's own copy, cut and paste stay native.
One table in src/common lists every prompt, editing, clipboard and app key with a description, and /keys shows it in both clients (artifact old-key-help.ts in task 1x), so the list cannot drift from what the keys do: a test checks that each listed binding is handled.`,
